-- PeaklyPrep: PeaklyTest Nov 2025 — full practice test from the PeaklyTest library (November 2025 form) --------
-- Generated from the PeaklyTest PDF: questions, answer choices, answer keys and explanations.
-- Figures live in public/q/nov25/. Ids are fixed, so running this file again updates the test instead of copying it.

-- PeaklyTest Nov 2025 · Reading & Writing · Module 1
insert into public.tests (id, title, section, description, duration_min, position, published, form_title, module_no)
values ('283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'PeaklyTest Nov 2025 · Reading & Writing · Module 1', 'Reading & Writing', 'Reading & Writing, Module 1 of the Nov 2025 PeaklyTest · 27 questions · 32 minutes', 32, 170, true, 'PeaklyTest Nov 2025 · Reading & Writing', 1)
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, section = excluded.section, description = excluded.description,
  duration_min = excluded.duration_min, position = excluded.position, form_title = excluded.form_title, module_no = excluded.module_no;
insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('c2ca93b5-62b5-5363-be63-f605226f6956', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["identify", "disturb", "replace", "exceed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A study by Augusta D. Gaspar and Joana Carneiro Pinto found that a bank’s corporate social responsibility (CSR) efforts, including environmental and social campaigns, improve its corporate image. When CSR was mentioned in bank marketing strategies, favorability scores assigned by study participants tended to ______ the scores assigned by participants when CSR wasn’t mentioned.'),
  ('5792f9d8-736e-52ea-b936-0d673bb73200', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["esoteric", "authoritative", "solitary", "speculative"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Researchers have long debated the origins of silver used in European coins from the 600s through the early 800s CE. Geochemical analysis by Kershaw et al. of 49 coins dating to 660–820 CE provides concrete evidence that reconciles two competing theories: early coins were made from Byzantine silver, and later coins used Frankish silver, findings that provide firm details in a previously ______ area of study.'),
  ('c2563253-978e-5a02-8dc6-8fcfdd7ecdeb', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'As used in the text, what does the word “mastery” most nearly mean?', '["Familiarity", "Domination", "Comprehension", "Skillfulness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The swift breezes on the beach at Pass Christian meet and conflict as though each strove for the <u>mastery</u> of the air. The land-breeze blows down through the pines, resinous, fragrant, cold, bringing breath-like memories of dim, dark woods shaded by myriad pine-needles. The breeze from the Gulf is warm and soft and languorous, blowing up from the south with its suggestion of tropical warmth.'),
  ('6b582347-a83b-5b08-968b-db1c65bb373e', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["diversion from", "catalyst of", "constraint to", "sponsor of"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Science fiction has long served as a ______ real-world technological advancements. Indeed, from Jules Verne’s 1865 novel From the Earth to the Moon inspiring developments in aerospace engineering to the television show Star Trek sparking the design of the ancestor of today’s smartphones, these narratives have spurred many actual innovations.'),
  ('e4ba5b47-a603-5372-b701-ad34d25292ca', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["exorbitant", "contentious", "equivocal", "warranted"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Any effort to raise the toll that drivers must pay to use the Lewis and Clark Bridge, which spans the Ohio River to connect Indiana and Kentucky, should explain why a higher toll is necessary ; no amount of justification, however, is likely to persuade some drivers who believe the current toll is _______.'),
  ('d7a79973-14c3-5293-af86-5a38ec6c1645', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?', '["It emphasizes the thoroughness of the research conducted.", "It explains how the new classification system was derived from the previous binary system.", "It justifies the need for a new classification system for marine species.", "It explains the basis for the new system’s scoring criteria."]'::jsonb, 6, 'Moving beyond a simple “present or absent” designation, researchers created a new classification system that allows for a nuanced understanding of bioluminescence in marine organisms that was unavailable under binary classification systems. <u>The new six-point scale considers varying levels of</u> <u>supporting evidence used to identify bioluminescent species</u>. For example, the firefly squid scores 1 because of inconsistent reports, the emperor squid scores 4 because of its anatomical similarity to known luminous relatives, and the shaggy anglerfish scores 6 because of substantiated direct observations.'),
  ('0d65f335-f96e-5321-a5f2-445db361dca4', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?', '["It presents information about the fossilization of pneumatic diverticula that has led paleontologists to question the applicability of studies of certain living species.", "It accounts for disagreements among paleontologists about how the respiratory systems of certain extinct genera functioned.", "It highlights an issue with fossils that previously led paleontologists to misidentify certain characteristics of pneumatic diverticula.", "It indicates why paleontologists have used another approach in addition to fossil analysis to learn about certain aspects of pneumatic diverticula."]'::jsonb, 7, 'Postcranial skeletal pneumaticity (PSP) refers to the presence of extensions of an animal’s lungs and air sacs inside its bones. These extensions are known as pneumatic diverticula. <u>Vascularity and other</u> <u>identifying characteristics of pneumatic diverticula tend not to fossilize</u>, so paleontologists have relied on studies of living bird species to document these qualities, augmenting their findings from fossil analysis to glean additional insights into the respiratory systems of extinct genera such as Tyrannosaurus, Euhelopus, and other Cretaceous theropods and sauropods that may have also exhibited PSP.'),
  ('677109c5-1548-55aa-bfb7-2edcef01c506', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?', '["It suggests that scientists are more concerned about other species than about glass eels’ habitat.", "It indicates that the benefit of understanding glass eels’ spawning behavior extends beyond the eels.", "It discusses a role that glass eels and other species serve in supporting the ecosystem of the Sargasso Sea.", "It presents a finding from a study that identifies the circumstances required to ensure the survival of glass eels."]'::jsonb, 8, 'Adult glass eels can be found off the coast of Maine, but the eels begin their lives in the Sargasso Sea, a biodiverse area in the North Atlantic Ocean where they are born and later return to breed. Though biologists believe they have identified the general area in the Sargasso Sea that is crucial to the endangered eels’ survival, little is yet known about how the animals spawn there. Scientists believe that solving the mystery will lead to better conservation of glass eels and their habitat, <u>helping in turn to</u> <u>sustain several other species that rely on them as a food source</u>.'),
  ('56f4f89b-bf32-538e-a184-8ae7bec7f38a', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'According to the text, what is someone who professionally evaluates books called?', '["A book publisher", "A literary critic", "A bookseller", "An author"]'::jsonb, 9, 'Seesaw Girl was Linda Sue Park’s debut novel. It was published in 1999. A debut novel is the first book that an author has published. Debut novels are especially interesting to literary critics (people whose job it is to evaluate books) and readers because these books offer a look at new voices in the literary world.'),
  ('d40ad783-68c6-5c32-9b82-4158bc3c075c', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?', '["0.38 million metric tons of copper.", "0.33 million metric tons of copper.", "1.85 million metric tons of copper.", "0.44 million metric tons of copper."]'::jsonb, 10, 'Millions of Metric Tons of Copper Mined in 1995 and 2020

| Country | 1995 | 2020 |
| Indonesia | 0.44 | 0.51 |
| Mexico | 0.33 | 0.73 |
| Peru | 0.38 | 2.15 |
| United States | 1.85 | 1.20 |

While doing research for a paper about metal exports, a student finds a table indicating how much copper was mined in each of four countries in 1995 and 2020. The student notes that in 1995, Mexico mined _____'),
  ('c20f8884-4989-5260-837d-64f796ddfa59', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which quotation from Peter Pan best supports the claim?', '["\"The rock was very small now.\"", "\"Wendy and John and Michael stood on tip-toe in the air to get their first sight of the island.\"", "\"Peter was alone on the lagoon.\"", "\"Feeling that Peter was on his way back, the Neverland had again woke into life.\""]'::jsonb, 11, 'Peter Pan is a 1911 novel by J.M. Barrie. In the fantasy novel, Peter is a young boy who can fly. He brings Wendy, John, and Michael to Neverland , the mythical island where he lives. The narrator suggests that activity on the island stops when Peter is away and starts again when he is about to return:'),
  ('6c55860f-f854-58ec-b70f-84e7e7e8f82c', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which quotation from a translation of The Underdogs most effectively illustrates the claim?', '["“Camilla stared up at the blue sky so [Luis] should not read the expression in her eyes.”", "“Camilla, standing on the beach of washed, round stones, caught a reflection of herself in the waters.”", "“All nature was as she had found it before, evening upon evening; but in the stones and the dry weeds, amid the fragrance of the air and the light whir of falling leaves, Camilla sensed a new strangeness, a vast desolation in everything about her.”", "“[Camilla] closed her eyes fast to hold back the tears that welled up in them. Then, with the back of her hand, she wiped her wet cheeks, and just as she had done three days ago, fled with all the swiftness of a young deer.”"]'::jsonb, 12, 'The Underdogs is a 1915 novel by Mariano Azuela, originally written in Spanish. In the novel, Azuela depicts the character Camilla as experiencing a change in how she perceives her immediate surroundings: _____'),
  ('d0f53970-4c1e-5f1c-a4e4-f7a17516e27a', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researcher’s claim?', '["A wider range of sounds is found across the languages of Africa as a whole than across the languages of South America as a whole.", "Languages that emerged in Europe and Western Asia tend to have more sounds than languages that emerged in Africa do.", "Languages that emerged in Central America tend to have fewer sounds than languages that emerged in Western Europe do, and Central America is farther away from Africa than Western Europe is.", "The number of sounds is fairly consistent across the various languages that emerged in the last parts of Africa to be settled by humans."]'::jsonb, 13, 'The Rarámuri language of northern Mexico has 20 vowel and consonant sounds. In contrast, the Taa language of southern Africa has over 100. Why would languages differ in this way? One researcher has claimed that when modern humans arose in Africa, they spoke a single language, but as humans gradually spread throughout Africa and then around the globe, that language developed into new languages. Those developed into still more languages as small bands of humans spread even farther, with each new language retaining fewer sounds from humanity’s original language.'),
  ('4c27512d-c24a-5691-b44b-f6e2ca528019', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["find listening to “Heartbeats” at 82.2 decibels less enjoyable than listening to it at a lower volume.", "prefer listening to other music at 82.2 decibels over listening to “Heartbeats” at that volume.", "enjoy listening to the nature sounds at 82.2 decibels more than listening to “Heartbeats” at 82.2 decibels.", "not find it enjoyable to listen to any recordings at a volume <u>as low as</u> 82.2 decibels."]'::jsonb, 14, 'Antonia Olivia Dolan and colleagues had musicians and nonmusicians with clinically average hearing listen to recordings of nature sounds and music in popular genres like acoustic folk and funk and adjust the volume to optimize their listening enjoyment. The researchers found that for a given recording that a musician and nonmusician identified as their favorite among those included in the study, optimal volume tended to be higher for the musician than for the nonmusician. Thus, if a musician and nonmusician both identified Jose Gonzalez’s “Heartbeats” as their favorite recording and the musician preferred to listen to it at a volume of 82.2 decibels, the nonmusician would be expected to ______'),
  ('b534de8b-49da-51c0-95d5-79ae2286cb79', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["narwhals forage at shallower depths in the presence of ship sounds alone than in the presence of ship sounds coupled with sonic pulses.", "sonic pulses can be heard at significantly greater ocean depths than ship sounds can.", "the narwhals weren’t as sensitive to human-caused sounds as the researchers had predicted.", "ship sounds contribute so much to the overall sound level that the addition of sonic pulses has little effect on the narwhals’ auditory environment."]'::jsonb, 15, 'Outi Tervo and team studied the effect of human-caused noise on narwhals (Monodon monoceros), arctic marine mammals that are sensitive to acoustic changes in their environment. Hypothesizing that elevated sound levels affect foraging among narwhals, Tervo’s team compared narwhal diving behaviors in natural sound conditions with those behaviors in two human-caused sound exposure conditions—ship sounds and ship sounds coupled with sonic pulses. Both exposure conditions resulted in significant decreases in the number and target depth of deep dives (associated with foraging) relative to natural conditions. However, differences between diving behaviors in the two exposure types were negligible, a finding that could be attributed to the fact that _____'),
  ('a3248e20-0fd8-575d-ad74-8df531336044', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["lost and conversely,", "lost, conversely,", "lost. Conversely,", "lost, and conversely"]'::jsonb, 16, 'Many works of the Greek mathematician Euclid (3rd century BCE) are _____ his Elements, a treatise of mathematical knowledge, is an extant work: it can still be read.'),
  ('e30cc7f4-87bf-57a6-953f-a938da72c5b1', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["recommended", "recommending", "to recommend", "having recommended"]'::jsonb, 17, 'A 2024 study cataloged all the trees in Amsterdam to evaluate the biodiversity of the city’s urban forest. The researchers, finding that administrative unit GK13 had a 0.62 ratio of native to non-native trees, ______ efforts to improve the biodiversity of the trees in that unit and other areas with a similarly moderate ratio.'),
  ('1e52db5d-d762-58ce-91ff-c2811d5d78c3', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["support. Decreasing", "support decreasing", "support, while decreasing", "support, decreasing"]'::jsonb, 18, 'Growing as large as three meters in diameter, the leaves of the giant Amazonian waterlily feature a complex network of radiating veins that provide structural ______ in thickness from the center to the edges, these veins allow the leaves to maintain their large size and buoyancy with minimal material, optimizing light capture and photosynthesis.'),
  ('fafa9fae-b607-5737-8758-7ffe092392da', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["Pennsylvania would officially become", "Pennsylvania had officially become", "Pennsylvania officially became", "Pennsylvania, having officially become"]'::jsonb, 19, '_____ a US state when it ratified the US Constitution on December 12, 1787, was thereby empowered, via its representatives to the US Congress, to vote on whether to admit Louisiana as a state on April 30, 1812.'),
  ('845f39f4-7227-541a-8b1d-45053cd7f940', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["has been revisited", "have been revisited", "revisited", "having been revisited"]'::jsonb, 20, 'Though largely eclipsed by her success as a novelist, Jessie Redmon Fauset’s contributions as literary editor of The Crisis, a New York City–based periodical whose influence was arguably at its peak during her Harlem Renaissance–era tenure, ______ in recent scholarship that explores Fauset’s instrumental role in shaping the magazine’s aesthetic and debuting the work of Langston Hughes and other emerging Black writers.'),
  ('6c2249ec-817f-5cfe-8e5d-ef9e1e0adf55', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["For example,", "However,", "Regardless,", "Next,"]'::jsonb, 21, 'Resins play several important roles in maintaining the health of conifers and many other kinds of trees. ______ resins quickly seal wounds, which helps prevent harmful insects and fungi from entering trees. These sticky substances also help trees retain water that is needed for them to survive.'),
  ('58081112-a9dd-5a29-ad6d-0074bc288790', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["On the other hand,", "Previously,", "For example,", "In other words,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'Giant dust plumes from the Sahara Desert that blow across the Atlantic Ocean can have complex and opposing effects on tropical cyclones. On one hand, the dust can enhance the formation of ice clouds in the cyclone’s core, increasing precipitation. ______ the dust can lower sea surface temperatures around the cyclone’s core, weakening the storm.'),
  ('64450bb7-c6ec-5101-8f64-d0ae42f95fdc', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["On the other hand,", "That is,", "For instance,", "There,"]'::jsonb, 23, 'Elvis Presley’s iconic jumpsuit Red Pinwheel featured rhinestones arranged in pinwheel patterns. Historians note that adornments such as these added between twenty-five and fifty pounds to each of Elvis’s stage outfits. ______ the fabric used to make the outfits was light and flexible, enabling freedom of motion.'),
  ('2275a007-5e44-5e0e-b4f3-6f42ae98a4a3', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["regardless;", "in other words;", "for instance;", "however;"]'::jsonb, 24, 'In a 1998 study by Smith et al., the researchers used microhistological fecal analysis to determine the ratio of three plant subtypes (graminoids, forbs, and browse) within the diets of North American ungulates. The researchers did not perform this analysis on all such ungulates, ______ they focused exclusively on pronghorns and wild horses in New Mexico.'),
  ('8d4042b6-10a5-5688-9d50-1e4c239b5bbb', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Specifically,", "Earlier,", "Hence,", "Similarly,"]'::jsonb, 25, 'Though its onboard laboratory allowed it to analyze rock samples on-site, the 2011 Mars Curiosity rover was unable to preserve samples for future analysis. ______ when creating the 2020 Mars Perseverance rover, robotics technologist Yumi Iwashita and other members of NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory sought to implement a mechanism that could do exactly that.'),
  ('3b832d28-5d0f-56b8-b025-cebc4de7706e', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Thus,", "By contrast,", "Even so,", "Nevertheless,"]'::jsonb, 26, 'As Iestyn Barr and his team of researchers discovered when establishing the glacial timeline of Antarctica, the Transantarctic Mountains—a 3,500-km mountain range spanning the continent—are home to glaciers of at least 60 million years in age. ______ the researchers concluded, Antarctica had glaciers long before the formation of its continent-wide ice sheet 34 million years ago.'),
  ('f5aadfdb-cdde-54dc-90e9-3f5194f6ae72', '283ea9a3-b735-569b-bd25-66ad47879bbc', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize Edison’s reliance on Hammer?', '["William Joseph Hammer, a laboratory assistant, worked at the Menlo Park laboratory.", "William Joseph Hammer, a laboratory assistant, was known for his valuable contribution to the field of technology.", "Most of Thomas Edison’s employees had successful careers independent of working for him; for example, William Joseph Hammer, who worked at the Menlo Park laboratory, was an accomplished laboratory assistant.", "Having received little formal education, Thomas Edison depended on specialized employees, including laboratory assistant William Joseph Hammer, for many of his inventions."]'::jsonb, 27, 'Thomas Edison is regarded as one of the most important inventors in US history.
Having received little formal schooling, Edison relied on specialist employees at his various laboratories and offices to complete the technical work for many of his inventions.
William Joseph Hammer was a laboratory assistant who worked at the Menlo Park laboratory. He is best known for curating the most comprehensive collection of historic light bulbs anywhere in the world.
William Symes Andrews was an electrical engineer who worked at the Edison Electric Light Company. He is best known for establishing more than thirty power plants across New York and Pennsylvania.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('c2ca93b5-62b5-5363-be63-f605226f6956', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage says CSR efforts “improve” a bank’s corporate image, so favorability scores when CSR was mentioned would be higher than when it wasn’t; to be higher is to “exceed” the other scores. The verb precisely matches the comparative relationship implied by “improve.”
**Choice A is incorrect.** “Identify” means to recognize or name and does not express a comparative increase in scores, so it does not fit the context of improved favorability.
**Choice B is incorrect.** “Disturb” implies causing upset or disruption, which contradicts the idea of improving a corporate image and making scores higher.
**Choice C is incorrect.** “Replace” means to take the place of, not to be greater than; it does not convey the intended comparative increase in favorability scores.
**Faster approach:** Spot the keyword "improve" and choose the option meaning "be higher than" (a comparative verb such as "exceed").'),
  ('5792f9d8-736e-52ea-b936-0d673bb73200', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence says the geochemical analysis "provides firm details" that reconcile competing theories; that implies the topic was previously uncertain or conjectural. "Speculative" precisely captures that sense of being based on conjecture rather than firm evidence, so the findings fill in a previously speculative area of study.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Esoteric" means intended for or likely to be understood by only a small number of people; it does not contrast with "firm details" in the way the sentence requires.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Authoritative" means possessing recognized accuracy or expertise; something previously authoritative would not be what new analysis needs to provide "firm details" to resolve.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Solitary" means alone or single; that sense is irrelevant to the idea of uncertainty or conjecture indicated by the sentence.
**Faster approach:** Look for the contrast with "firm details" — the blank should describe the prior state (uncertain/guess-based), so choose the word meaning "uncertain or conjectural" (speculative).'),
  ('c2563253-978e-5a02-8dc6-8fcfdd7ecdeb', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** In the sentence the breezes “meet and conflict as though each strove for the mastery of the air,” the verb phrase “strove for” indicates competition to control or rule the air. "Mastery" therefore most nearly means domination, the idea of one breeze prevailing over the other.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Familiarity means being well-acquainted with something; that sense does not fit the imagery of the breezes struggling against one another.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Comprehension means understanding; nothing in the context implies the breezes are trying to understand the air.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Skillfulness means adeptness or expertise; while related to mastery in some contexts, here the competitive, adversarial phrasing points to domination rather than mere skill.
**Faster approach:** Use the verbs around the word (e.g., “strove for,” “conflict”) to determine whether the sense is competitive/control (domination) or something like knowledge/skill; choose the meaning consistent with that competition.'),
  ('6b582347-a83b-5b08-968b-db1c65bb373e', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence says science fiction has prompted concrete technological developments; calling it a "catalyst" appropriately conveys that it stimulates or accelerates real-world innovation. The surrounding examples (Verne inspiring aerospace engineering and Star Trek influencing smartphone design) support the idea of causing or encouraging advances.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Diversion from" suggests a distraction away from technological advancements, which contradicts the examples showing influence toward such developments.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Constraint to" implies limiting or restricting technological progress, which conflicts with the passage’s examples of inspiration and impetus.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Sponsor of" means financially supporting innovations; the passage emphasizes inspirational influence rather than funding.
**Faster approach:** Quickly eliminate options that contradict the examples (words implying restriction or distraction), then choose the remaining option whose meaning—"catalyst"—matches "inspiring or accelerating" developments.'),
  ('e4ba5b47-a603-5372-b701-ad34d25292ca', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The phrase "no amount of justification... is likely to persuade" implies that some drivers already hold a negative judgment about the current toll that would make them resistant to arguments for an increase. "Exorbitant" means excessively high; drivers who think the toll is exorbitant would not be swayed by explanations for raising it, so this choice best completes the sentence.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Contentious" means controversial or likely to cause disagreement; it doesn’t express a value judgment (too high) that would explain why drivers are impervious to justification.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Equivocal" means ambiguous or unclear, which doesn’t explain why drivers wouldn’t be persuaded by further justification.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Warranted" means justified or appropriate; that is essentially the opposite of the negative judgment signaled by the sentence, so it doesn’t fit the context.
**Faster approach:** Quickly plug each choice into the sentence; the signal "no amount of justification" indicates the blank should express a strong negative judgment (e.g., "exorbitant"), so eliminate choices that don’t convey that meaning.'),
  ('d7a79973-14c3-5293-af86-5a38ec6c1645', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The underlined sentence states that the six-point scale "considers varying levels of supporting evidence used to identify bioluminescent species," which explicitly describes the basis on which the system assigns scores. This directly explains the scoring criteria and is reinforced by the examples that follow (different species receiving scores based on types of evidence).
**Choice A is incorrect.** The sentence does not stress the thoroughness of the research; it describes what the scale takes into account, not the researchers'' rigor.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The sentence does not describe derivation from a previous binary system; it specifies what the new scale evaluates.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The sentence does not argue for the need for a new system; it explains how the system assigns scores.
**Faster approach:** Look for wording that states what the system "considers" or "uses"—that wording signals an explanation of scoring criteria and points to choice D.'),
  ('0d65f335-f96e-5321-a5f2-445db361dca4', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The underlined clause explains that vascularity and other identifying features "tend not to fossilize," and immediately connects that fact to paleontologists'' reliance on studies of living birds to document those qualities. In other words, it gives the reason paleontologists used an additional approach beyond fossils, which matches choice D.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The sentence does not say that paleontologists questioned the applicability of studies of living species; it states that certain features don’t fossilize and therefore living-species studies were used to document those features, not that the studies were questioned.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The clause does not describe disagreements among scientists about respiratory function; it explains a methodological reason for using living birds as supplementary evidence.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The text does not claim fossils led to misidentifications; it only notes that some features don’t preserve and so researchers consult living species to learn about those missing characteristics.
**Faster approach:** Locate the cause-and-effect connector ("so") in the sentence to identify that it explains why a different method was used; then choose the option that states that reason directly.'),
  ('677109c5-1548-55aa-bfb7-2edcef01c506', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The underlined clause explains a broader payoff of solving the eels'' spawning mystery: conserving glass eels would also support other species that feed on them. This shows that the benefit "extends beyond the eels," which directly matches the sentence’s purpose in the passage.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The clause does not claim scientists care more about other species; it states a consequence of conservation, not scientists'' priorities.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The clause does not describe the ecological roles of multiple species or detail ecosystem support; it simply notes that other species rely on glass eels as food.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The clause does not present a study finding or identify specific survival requirements for glass eels; it states an expected benefit of solving the spawning mystery.
**Faster approach:** Look for language indicating scope of impact (words like "helping," "in turn," "also")—that pattern signals the sentence is extending the benefit beyond the main subject, which points directly to choice B.'),
  ('56f4f89b-bf32-538e-a184-8ae7bec7f38a', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage defines the term directly: it says "literary critics (people whose job it is to evaluate books)," so someone who professionally evaluates books is a literary critic.
**Choice A is incorrect.** A book publisher is someone who produces and distributes books; the passage does not equate publishers with people who evaluate books.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A bookseller sells books; the passage’s parenthetical definition refers to evaluating books, not selling them.
**Choice D is incorrect.** An author writes books; while authors may evaluate books, the passage specifically names "literary critics" as the professionals whose job is to evaluate books.
**Faster approach:** Look for the parenthetical definition next to the term—many questions of this type are answered by matching the defined phrase to the choices.'),
  ('d40ad783-68c6-5c32-9b82-4158bc3c075c', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The table lists Mexico’s copper production in 1995 as 0.33 million metric tons, so the most effective completion is "0.33 million metric tons of copper." This directly uses the value from the table.
**Choice A is incorrect.** 0.38 million metric tons is the 1995 value for Peru, not Mexico, so it does not match the table entry for Mexico.
**Choice C is incorrect.** 1.85 million metric tons is the 1995 value for the United States, not Mexico, so it is not the correct figure for Mexico.
**Choice D is incorrect.** 0.44 million metric tons is the 1995 value for Indonesia, not Mexico, so it does not correctly complete the statement about Mexico.
**Faster approach:** Scan the table to the row labeled "Mexico" and read the 1995 column; use that exact numeric entry to complete the sentence.'),
  ('c20f8884-4989-5260-837d-64f796ddfa59', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence "Feeling that Peter was on his way back, the Neverland had again woke into life." directly states that the island becomes active again when Peter is about to return, which matches the claim that activity stops when he is away and resumes as he comes back.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "The rock was very small now." describes a visual detail and does not address island activity or Peter’s presence.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Wendy and John and Michael stood on tip-toe in the air to get their first sight of the island." describes the children’s view of the island, not the island’s activity in relation to Peter.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Peter was alone on the lagoon." only notes Peter’s location and solitude, and does not indicate that the island’s activity changes when he is absent or returning.
**Faster approach:** For evidence questions, scan the passage for a sentence that restates the claim in similar language; choose the quotation that explicitly links Peter’s presence with the island’s activity.'),
  ('6c55860f-f854-58ec-b70f-84e7e7e8f82c', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The quotation explicitly states that Camilla "sensed a new strangeness, a vast desolation in everything about her," which directly describes a change in how she perceives her surroundings. The language signals a shift from prior perception to a newly felt alienation in familiar elements of nature.
**Choice A is incorrect.** That line describes Camilla hiding her expression from Luis; it shows deliberate concealment of feeling, not a change in how she perceives her environment.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Seeing her reflection is a concrete action but does not indicate a changed perception of the world around her.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Her closing her eyes and fleeing "as she had done three days ago" emphasizes repetition of past behavior rather than a new way of perceiving her surroundings.
**Faster approach:** Look for wording that signals change (phrases like "new," "strangeness," "desolation") and eliminate choices that describe actions or repetition without perceptual shift.'),
  ('d0f53970-4c1e-5f1c-a4e4-f7a17516e27a', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The researcher’s claim predicts that languages closer to humanity’s African origin should retain more sounds, and languages that arose farther away should retain fewer. If languages that emerged in Europe and Western Asia tend to have more sounds than languages that emerged in Africa, that directly contradicts this predicted pattern and therefore most strongly weakens the claim.
**Choice A is incorrect.** A wider range of sounds within Africa than within South America does not contradict the claim; it could be consistent with greater original diversity in Africa.
**Choice C is incorrect.** This choice is aligned with the researcher’s claim (languages farther from Africa—Central America—have fewer sounds than those nearer—Western Europe) and thus does not weaken it.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Consistency of sound counts within late-settled parts of Africa may limit a within-Africa gradient but does not directly contradict the broader claim about decreasing sounds with successive outward dispersals.
**Faster approach:** Identify the claim’s predicted correlation (distance from origin → fewer sounds) and choose the option that directly contradicts that correlation (shows more sounds farther from the origin).'),
  ('4c27512d-c24a-5691-b44b-f6e2ca528019', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage reports that for the same recording a musician’s optimal volume tended to be higher than a nonmusician’s. If the musician’s preferred level for “Heartbeats” is 82.2 decibels, the nonmusician would—by that general finding—prefer a lower volume; therefore the nonmusician would find 82.2 decibels less enjoyable than a lower volume that is closer to their own optimum.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage compares preferred volumes for the same recording, not preferences between different recordings at a fixed volume, so it does not support preferring other music at 82.2 dB.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The study compared optimal volumes for music and nature sounds separately but the specific finding described relates to relative optimal volumes for the same recording; it does not imply the nonmusician would prefer nature sounds to that piece at 82.2 dB.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This statement contradicts the passage: the nonmusician is expected to prefer a lower volume than 82.2 dB, not fail to enjoy any volume as low as 82.2 dB.
**Faster approach:** Translate the passage’s comparative statement into a simple inequality (musician’s optimal volume > nonmusician’s); then plug in the given number (82.2) to see which choice asserts that the nonmusician prefers a lower volume.'),
  ('b534de8b-49da-51c0-95d5-79ae2286cb79', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage says both exposure conditions produced similar decreases in deep dives, so a logical explanation is that adding sonic pulses didn’t change the narwhals’ auditory environment much because ship sounds already dominated the sound level. That account directly explains why the two different exposure types produced negligible behavioral differences.
**Choice A is incorrect.** If narwhals foraged at shallower depths with ship sounds alone than with ship sounds plus pulses, the two exposure types would produce different behaviors; that contradicts the passage’s statement that differences were negligible.
**Choice B is incorrect.** If sonic pulses reached greater depths than ship sounds, that would likely produce additional behavioral differences between the two exposures, not negligible differences.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage reports significant decreases in deep dives under exposure, so narwhals do respond to human-caused sounds; this choice doesn’t explain why the two exposure types produced similar effects.
**Faster approach:** For inference questions comparing two experimental conditions, pick the answer that explains similarity by identifying a dominant factor that makes the added variable negligible (i.e., one condition already overwhelms the effect of the other).'),
  ('a3248e20-0fd8-575d-ad74-8df531336044', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence needs a strong boundary before the transitional adverb "Conversely" because the clause that follows is an independent clause: "his Elements ... is an extant work: it can still be read." A period correctly ends the first independent clause ("Many works ... are lost.") and allows "Conversely," to introduce the contrasting statement.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "lost and conversely," incorrectly connects two independent clauses with only a coordinating conjunction plus a comma; the structure is awkward and ungrammatical because "conversely" needs to introduce a contrasting clause, not join with "and."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "lost, conversely," creates a comma splice by joining two independent clauses with only a comma and the adverb "conversely," which does not serve as a coordinating conjunction.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "lost, and conversely" also misuses the coordinating conjunction "and" with "conversely," producing an awkward, nonstandard construction; a period before "Conversely" is required for proper contrast.
**Faster approach:** When a transitional adverb like "Conversely" begins a contrasting independent clause, put a period (or semicolon) before it; choose the option that creates two properly punctuated sentences.'),
  ('e30cc7f4-87bf-57a6-953f-a938da72c5b1', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The main clause of the sentence needs a finite past-tense verb to show the action the researchers took after discovering the ratio, and "recommended" properly completes that clause: "The researchers ... recommended efforts to improve..." The participial phrase "finding that ..." correctly modifies the subject "The researchers," and the past-tense verb indicates the resulting action.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Recommending" is a present participle and would create a sentence fragment or verb form mismatch; it does not provide a finite main verb for the clause.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "To recommend" is an infinitive and would not serve as the main finite verb for the clause, producing an ungrammatical sentence.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Having recommended" is a perfect participle implying the recommendation occurred before the finding; that reverses the intended sequence and fails to supply a simple past main verb.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the sentence already has a main verb; if not, choose a finite verb (usually past tense here) that fits the subject—so pick the past tense form "recommended."'),
  ('1e52db5d-d762-58ce-91ff-c2811d5d78c3', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence as written contains two independent ideas: the veins "provide structural support" and the fact that they "decrease in thickness from the center to the edges"; joining those with only a comma would create a run-on (comma splice). Option A avoids that error by ending the first sentence with a period and beginning a new participial phrase, "Decreasing in thickness from the center to the edges, these veins allow...," which correctly modifies the subject and yields a grammatical, clear pair of sentences.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "support decreasing" leaves the sentence as a run-on and makes "support" seem to be modified by "decreasing," which is syntactically awkward and ungrammatical here.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Inserting "while" creates a clumsy construction that still produces a comma splice and mislinks the clauses, failing to resolve the sentence-boundary problem.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Using a single comma before "decreasing" produces a comma splice by joining two independent clauses without an appropriate conjunction or separation; the modifier placement is also less clear than in A.
**Faster approach:** Watch for comma splices—if a sentence seems to have two independent clauses joined only by a comma, prefer a period or a proper conjunction; that quick check usually yields the correct choice on sentence-boundary problems.'),
  ('fafa9fae-b607-5737-8758-7ffe092392da', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence requires an introductory modifier describing Pennsylvania’s status at the time, followed by a single main clause ("was thereby empowered"). "Pennsylvania, having officially become a US state when it ratified the US Constitution on December 12, 1787, was thereby empowered..." correctly forms a participial phrase that modifies "Pennsylvania" and leaves "was" as the sentence’s main verb.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Pennsylvania would officially become" creates a future/conditional construction that conflicts with the past context and leaves two finite verbs without proper connection.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Pennsylvania had officially become" places a past-perfect finite verb before the main clause, producing an ungrammatical sequence (two finite verbs for one subject) without a conjunction.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Pennsylvania officially became" also inserts a finite verb where the sentence needs a single main verb later, resulting in a run-on structure.
**Faster approach:** For items testing sentence structure, mentally insert each choice and check that the sentence has exactly one main finite verb and that any introductory phrase correctly modifies the subject; eliminate choices that introduce an extra finite verb or create a dangling modifier.'),
  ('845f39f4-7227-541a-8b1d-45053cd7f940', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The grammatical subject of the clause is "contributions," which is plural, so the verb must also be plural; the present perfect passive "have been revisited" correctly matches number and tense and fits smoothly into the sentence: "...contributions... have been revisited in recent scholarship..."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Has been revisited" uses a singular verb form that does not agree with the plural subject "contributions."
**Choice C is incorrect.** The bare past "revisited" would create a misplaced or truncated construction here and does not provide the necessary passive voice linking the contributions to recent scholarship in the same way the present perfect passive does.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Having been revisited" produces a dangling participial phrase that would incorrectly modify Jessie Redmon Fauset rather than her "contributions," creating an illogical sentence structure.
**Faster approach:** Identify the grammatical subject ("contributions" = plural) and choose a plural verb form in the correct voice; for passive constructions describing research, the present perfect passive "have been revisited" is the standard concise choice.'),
  ('6c2249ec-817f-5cfe-8e5d-ef9e1e0adf55', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence after the blank gives a specific way resins function: they "quickly seal wounds," which illustrates one of the "important roles" mentioned in the first sentence. "For example," appropriately introduces a specific instance of the general claim and connects smoothly to the following explanatory clause about sealing wounds.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "However," signals a contrast, but the sentence after the blank does not contradict the first sentence; it provides a supporting example, so a contrast marker is inappropriate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Regardless," implies that what follows is true despite something previously stated; the wound-sealing detail is not presented as independent of or contrary to the initial claim, so this transition is wrong.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Next," indicates a sequence of steps or events; the passage is giving an example of a role, not listing sequential steps, so "Next," does not fit.
**Faster approach:** Look for signal words in the surrounding sentences (e.g., "for example" for a specific instance, "however" for a contrast, "next" for sequence); match the transition to the rhetorical relationship between sentences.'),
  ('58081112-a9dd-5a29-ad6d-0074bc288790', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage sets up a contrast between two opposing effects of Saharan dust on tropical cyclones: enhancing ice-cloud formation (increasing precipitation) versus weakening the storm by lowering sea surface temperatures. The transition "On the other hand," signals this contrast and fits the sentence both logically and grammatically.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Previously," indicates a time sequence, which does not match the intended contrast between two simultaneous or alternative effects.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "For example," would introduce an illustration or specific case of the first effect, but the second clause presents a contrasting effect, not an example.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "In other words," restates or clarifies the prior idea, whereas the sentence requires a word that signals opposition.
**Faster approach:** Look for signal words showing contrast in the surrounding sentences; use "on the other hand" when the passage describes opposing effects.'),
  ('64450bb7-c6ec-5101-8f64-d0ae42f95fdc', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence contrasts the heavy adornments (adding twenty-five to fifty pounds) with the fact that the fabric itself was light and flexible, so a transitional phrase that signals contrast is required. "On the other hand," appropriately introduces that contrasting idea: although the embellishments were heavy, the underlying fabric allowed freedom of motion.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "That is," signals a restatement or clarification of the previous idea, but the second clause presents a contrasting point, not a clarification, so this choice does not fit.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "For instance," introduces an example of the previous statement; here the sentence does not offer an example of the adornments but rather a contrasting fact, so this choice is inappropriate.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "There," is ungrammatical and does not serve as a transition that creates the needed contrast, so it fails to connect the two clauses logically.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the second clause contrasts, restates, or gives an example of the first; pick a transition that matches that relationship (contrast → "On the other hand/However", restatement → "That is", example → "For instance").'),
  ('2275a007-5e44-5e0e-b4f3-6f42ae98a4a3', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence sets up a contrast between the general possibility (performing the analysis on all ungulates) and the actual, narrower practice (focusing only on pronghorns and wild horses). "However" signals that contrast most clearly: "They did not perform this analysis on all such ungulates; however, they focused exclusively on..."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Regardless" means "despite that" and does not create the clear contrast needed here; it would make the second clause sound independent rather than contrasting.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "In other words" signals a restatement or clarification of the same idea, but the second clause provides a contrasting limitation, not a restatement.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "For instance" introduces an example, which would imply that pronghorns and wild horses are merely examples of many studied species, but the sentence indicates exclusivity rather than example.
**Faster approach:** When a sentence contrasts a general expectation with a narrower reality, choose a contrast word like "however"; if the second clause restates, choose "in other words," and if it gives an example, choose "for instance."'),
  ('8d4042b6-10a5-5688-9d50-1e4c239b5bbb', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage states that the 2011 Curiosity rover could not preserve samples; the next clause explains that, in response, designers of the 2020 Perseverance rover sought to implement a mechanism that could preserve samples. "Hence" signals a causal or consequential relationship and correctly links the inability of Curiosity to the action taken for Perseverance.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Specifically" narrows or gives detail about something just mentioned, but the second clause describes a response to Curiosity’s shortcoming rather than a further detail about that shortcoming.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Earlier" indicates chronology preceding the prior statement; here the sentence describes a subsequent response, not something that happened earlier.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Similarly" indicates that the second clause is like the first, but the second clause actually presents a corrective action, not a parallel situation.
**Faster approach:** For transitions, identify whether the sentence shows cause/result (use
"hence/consequently"), contrast ("however"), sequence ("earlier/afterward"), or example/detail ("specifically").'),
  ('3b832d28-5d0f-56b8-b025-cebc4de7706e', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence before the blank states a finding (glaciers at least 60 million years old), and the clause after the blank states the researchers'' conclusion based on that finding (that Antarctica had glaciers long before the continent-wide ice sheet formed 34 million years ago). "Thus," signals a logical result or conclusion drawn from prior information, so it correctly connects the evidence to the researchers'' conclusion.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "By contrast," signals a comparison showing difference; nothing in the surrounding text presents a contrasting idea to the finding, so this transition is inappropriate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Even so," implies an unexpected or contrary result despite what was just said; the conclusion is a straightforward inference, not a surprising exception.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Nevertheless," also implies concession or contrast, which does not match the purely inferential relationship between the evidence and the conclusion here.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the second clause states a consequence, contrast, or concession relative to the first; pick a transition that matches that logical relationship (here, a consequence → "Thus").'),
  ('f5aadfdb-cdde-54dc-90e9-3f5194f6ae72', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** It directly combines the passage’s points that Edison had little formal schooling and that he “relied on specialist employees” by stating that Edison “depended on specialized employees, including laboratory assistant William Joseph Hammer, for many of his inventions,” which explicitly emphasizes Hammer’s role in supporting Edison.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It merely restates that Hammer worked at Menlo Park without linking him to Edison’s reliance on specialist employees.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does not say Hammer made a broadly “valuable contribution to the field of technology,” so this overstates the given information.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It shifts focus to employees’ independent careers and does not emphasize Edison’s dependence on Hammer; it also adds an unsupported generalization about most employees.
**Faster approach:** Look for the answer that explicitly links Edison’s reliance on specialists to Hammer; eliminate choices that only restate facts or introduce unsupported claims.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;

-- PeaklyTest Nov 2025 · Reading & Writing · Module 2
insert into public.tests (id, title, section, description, duration_min, position, published, form_title, module_no)
values ('03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'PeaklyTest Nov 2025 · Reading & Writing · Module 2', 'Reading & Writing', 'Reading & Writing, Module 2 of the Nov 2025 PeaklyTest · 27 questions · 32 minutes', 32, 171, true, 'PeaklyTest Nov 2025 · Reading & Writing', 2)
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, section = excluded.section, description = excluded.description,
  duration_min = excluded.duration_min, position = excluded.position, form_title = excluded.form_title, module_no = excluded.module_no;
insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('7d4a22a4-b970-5c57-88b1-a286a5705ead', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["preliminary", "clandestine", "unequivocal", "inexplicable"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Louisa May Alcott contributed A Modern Mephistopheles to the No Name Series, a collection of novels published anonymously between 1876 and 1887. The series challenged readers to guess authors'' identities, reflecting the era’s fascination for literary mystery. This ______ approach to publishing allowed Alcott to experiment beyond her established style.'),
  ('9e633301-1259-5cb8-90a7-a3ba8da0b331', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["surreptitiously", "disingenuously", "succinctly", "dispassionately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Though copies of The Adventures of Indiana Jones in Wenceslas Square in Prague on January 16, 1989—an underground computer game that was created anonymously in 1989 as an act of political protest against the authoritarian regime of what was then Czechoslovakia—were originally distributed _______, the game is now readily available online for anyone to play.'),
  ('e36ea62e-4f51-538a-9f9d-24e8631ba8e0', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["engenders", "preempts", "subsumes", "attenuates"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In their study of caterpillars in fragmented forests (formerly contiguous forests that have been broken into isolated patches by roads or other interruptions), Riley M. Anderson et al. stress that although fragmentation inevitably ______ alterations to local ecological processes, the changes will likely have a greater impact on Nola triquetrana, a specialist feeding on a limited number of plant species, than on a species like Himelia intractata that feeds on several.'),
  ('c6c551f4-bca3-580c-8285-65bfa56eb5fb', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["belies", "portends", "thwarts", "maligns"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Yuen Yuen Ang et al. argue that merely tallying the number of patents filed per year ______ the degree of innovation occurring: truly novel patents—those, like a 2011 patent for an automated navigation system for planes, that combine previously unaffiliated technological domains—are vastly outnumbered by nonnovel patents (e.g., a 2008 patent for a method of making soup).'),
  ('ee477269-05a6-5d86-ab6f-8b389bc28a93', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence?', '["To identify an unrecognized point of similarity between Chronicle of a Death Foretold and So Far from God", "To suggest that Mexican American authors who were influenced by García Márquez may have, in turn, influenced him", "To refute the possible impression that García Márquez conformed to magical realism in all of his works", "To challenge the common assumption that One Hundred Years of Solitude is superior to realist works by García Márquez"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Magical realism, a style that weaves fantasy into realistic narratives, coalesced in Latin America in the 1960s. It is associated with Gabriel García Márquez, whose One Hundred Years of Solitude is a key text. The style was embraced by Mexican American writers, such as Ana Castillo, whose So Far from God draws on Mexican folktales as a source of fantasy. Yet The Mixquiahuala Letters, also by Castillo, features no fantastical plot events. <u>And despite his popular reputation, García Márquez himself wrote</u> <u>several works of strict realism, including Chronicle of a Death Foretold.</u>'),
  ('d2e449e8-3e72-5c21-8f84-d6baaf41ff0c', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?', '["Text 2 describes a methodology that helps researchers avoid a problem encountered in the studies discussed in Text 1.", "Text 2 explains a study that suggests an alternative explanation for the trend observed in the research discussed in Text 1.", "Text 2 discusses an observation that challenges the validity of the findings described in Text 1.", "Text 2 presents a finding that casts doubt on the generalizability of the research discussed in Text 1."]'::jsonb, 6, '**Text 1**

Sidebells wintergreen (Orthilia secunda) plants are native to Alaska, where harsh conditions have historically impeded potential invasive species. As the boreal climate has warmed in recent decades, however, Siberian peashrub (Caragana arborescens) plants have established themselves in Alaska. Previous research conducted in non-boreal ecosystems has documented warming-induced increases in summer temperatures benefiting invasive species more than native species.

**Text 2**

At a site near Fairbanks, Alaska, Christa Mulder and Katie Spellman tracked O. secunda and C. arborescens, along with other native and invasive species, over several years. They observed that although average summer temperatures were substantially higher in some years of the study than in others, neither O. secunda nor C. arborescens showed any significant variation in summer growth patterns from year to year.'),
  ('08582153-53d1-505f-9374-3fd1d34e548a', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'What does the text most directly suggest about translation apps?', '["They have improved remarkably over time but remain insufficient to support the complexity called for in certain interactions.", "They are coming to be embraced by international tourists but are viewed with skepticism by many business professionals.", "They are becoming simpler to use but are inconsistent in how comprehensively they cover different languages.", "They have gained impressive capabilities but continue to be widely viewed as inadequate for most practical purposes."]'::jsonb, 7, 'Linguist John McWhorter asserts that translation apps for smartphones and computers are—despite generally failing to convey many nuances—increasingly obviating the need to learn new languages. Advances in language processing technology have greatly boosted the utility of these apps for perfunctory tasks, like inquiring about an item on a menu, and passing interactions; be that as it may, richer communication (e.g., in business dealings or meaningful personal exchanges) often hinges on conversational patterns and gradations of meaning.'),
  ('0d73977e-aecc-568c-9737-837a93d9782b', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Information in the text best supports which statement about Mohol bushbabies?', '["If increased lunar intensity imposed the same costs on the bushbabies that it imposes on white-throated woodrats, there would be no association between lunar intensity and the bushbabies'' foraging.", "If the advantages that the bushbabies gain from increased lunar intensity explain the change in their foraging behavior, those advantages are likely shared by some other species that are not heavily reliant on vision.", "If increased lunar intensity creates any disadvantages for the bushbabies, those disadvantages are more than compensated for by the advantages that the bushbabies gain.", "If the bushbabies'' foraging behavior under increased lunar intensity actually reflects a cost-benefit trade-off, their behavior should be more similar to that of white-throated woodrats than it is."]'::jsonb, 8, 'Optimal foraging theory (OFT) holds that animals'' foraging behaviors reflect cost-benefit trade-offs that vary by species and with dynamic ecological circumstances. One such circumstance is lunar intensity, which Mary V. Price and colleagues found to be negatively associated with foraging by white-throated woodrats but Simon Kenneth Bearder and colleagues found to be positively associated with foraging by Mohol bushbabies. This discrepancy is explicable in terms of OFT: the bushbabies'' greater reliance on vision means that higher lunar intensity benefits them more than it benefits the woodrats.'),
  ('7b21bfb3-781e-5978-ba9f-6ff6740bd793', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?', '["biochar and NPK fertilizer.", "biochar and compost.", "biochar alone.", "compost alone."]'::jsonb, 9, '**Effect of Various Soil Treatments on Mean Pineapple Fruit Weight and Size**

| Soil treatment | Weight (grams) | Length (centimeters) | Diameter (centimeters) |
| Control | 825.9 | 6.14 | 13.63 |
| Biochar | 915.7 | 6.56 | 13.63 |
| Compost | 864.8 | 6.15 | 13.22 |
| Biochar and compost | 979.3 | 6.76 | 13.68 |
| Biochar and NPK fertilizer | 1032.1 | 6.78 | 13.96 |

Working in Ghana, Emmanuel Hanyabui and colleagues compared the impact on pineapple growth of different combinations of soil additives, including NPK fertilizer (an inorganic fertilizer containing nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium), organic compost, and biochar (a carbon-rich material produced from organic waste matter). Based on data in the table, pineapple farmers with no access to inorganic soil additives would likely increase the weight and size of their fruits by the greatest amount by using _____'),
  ('2eac602f-34ea-524a-8681-3f1cb6b07452', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice best presents a conclusion about the habits of New Year’s resolution makers that is best supported by information in the text and the table?', '["The majority of US adults who make resolutions related to health and exercise also make resolutions in multiple additional categories.", "Resolution makers between the ages of 50 and 64 are more likely to make resolutions related to personal relationships and less likely to make resolutions related to finances than resolution makers between the ages of 30 and 49 are.", "Among all US adults, people become less likely to make New Year’s resolutions as they age, regardless of the type of resolution.", "Resolution makers between the ages of 18 and 29 are more likely to make resolutions about health and exercise than resolution makers between the ages of 30 and 49 are."]'::jsonb, 10, '**Percentages of New Year’s Resolution Makers Who Make Certain Kinds of Resolutions**

| Type of resolution | Age 18–29 | Age 30–49 | Age 50–64 | Age 65+ |
| Health and exercise | 79 | 80 | 79 | 76 |
| Finances | 68 | 63 | 56 | 47 |
| Personal relationships | 63 | 53 | 58 | 52 |
| Hobbies | 65 | 53 | 51 | 45 |

A Pew Research Center survey conducted in January 2024 found that three out of ten US adults make at least one New Year’s resolution (a promise for the year ahead), while half of those who make a resolution make more than one. The survey asked participants what kinds of resolutions they made and separated them into several categories. The table presents percentages of people who make particular kinds of New Year’s resolutions among those who choose to make them, indexed by age bracket.'),
  ('78eab20a-5b84-5bb3-aaa3-a2f85715bd36', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which quotation from a literary scholar would most directly support the claim in the underlined portion of the text?', '["“Much of the interest of Beloved derives from the productive tension between its competing influences—namely, Black American cultural traditions and the magical realism of Latin America.”", "“The cultural traditions of the Black American community, which figure so prominently in the magical realist tradition of Latin America, permit realistic as well as antirealistic scenarios—much as Beloved does.”", "“Even though Beloved alternates between realistic and antirealistic modes of representation, the influence of Black American cultural traditions remains constant throughout the novel.”", "“Although much of Beloved conforms to the conventions of realistic fiction, Toni Morrison also incorporates elements drawn from Black American cultural traditions that transcend and expose the limitations of realism.”"]'::jsonb, 11, 'Scholars cite One Hundred Years of Solitude , the 1967 novel by Colombian author Gabriel García Márquez, as a foundational text of magical realism, the Latin American style of fiction in which antirealistic plot devices—often borrowed from the spiritual and narrative traditions of Indigenous and colonial societies in the Americas—are deployed in an otherwise realistic mode of representation typical of the modern novel. This style has exerted a decisive influence on authors in the United States, including Toni Morrison, <u>whose 1987 novel Beloved resembles classic magical</u> <u>realist novels in its juxtaposition of literary realism with long-established cultural traditions—namely,</u> <u>those of the Black American community.</u>'),
  ('0d236575-8087-5ff9-91ae-f55f21f057ea', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["if each ecotype is indeed locally adapted as the researchers hypothesized, those adaptations are to other environmental conditions than the water each ecotype inhabits.", "while the ecotypes are genetically and geographically distinct, those differences do not represent adaptations to local environmental conditions.", "there may not be significant differences in the water that each ecotype inhabits, but there are significant differences in each ecotype’s resistance to zinc pollution.", "although the researchers’ hypothesis does not appear to be supported, this may be because the levels of zinc exposure the plants in the experiment received did not match their exposure in their natural environments."]'::jsonb, 12, 'Duckweed is a small freshwater plant that is often exposed to zinc pollution. Sofia Vámos and colleagues collected samples of four duckweed ecotypes (genetically and geographically distinct populations within a species), along with water from each ecotype’s habitat. Hypothesizing that each ecotype is adapted to its local conditions in ways that bolster its growth and resistance to pollutants, the researchers grew each ecotype in all four water samples and with three levels of zinc (none, low, high). (The researchers did not replicate local differences in light or temperature.) They found that the ecotypes grew equally well in all four water samples and that adding zinc consistently enhanced growth, regardless of concentration, suggesting that _____'),
  ('2c63cd87-7845-5a42-8f7c-03ce37be0ab3', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["the percentage of blue-feathered females will increase until fewer than half of adult female jacobins are green-feathered.", "the occurrence of blue head plumage in adult female jacobins is driven by one or more factors not associated with mate attraction.", "coloration prevents green-feathered adult female jacobins from distinguishing between adult males and blue-feathered adult females.", "adult male jacobins do not act antagonistically toward juvenile jacobins with blue head plumage when competing for resources."]'::jsonb, 13, 'As juveniles, all white-necked jacobin hummingbirds display vibrantly blue head plumage; when they enter adulthood, males retain these blue feathers and most females molt to a drab green hue. However, 28% of adult female jacobins remain identical in coloration to juveniles and adult males. Based on field observations in Panama, a team of researchers reports that while adult males show a clear preference in mate selection for adult females with drab green feathers, they also engage in more antagonistic behavior toward those adult females than toward blue-feathered adult females when competing for resources. Therefore, the team hypothesizes that'),
  ('59f1afad-f67e-5105-b598-3f72bf1ca1c9', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["century and", "century", "century,", "century;"]'::jsonb, 14, 'In Puerto Rico, it’s not unusual for a city or town to be known by a nickname that corresponds to one of its notable features, like landscape, climate, famous residents, or chief export. For example, the Puerto Rican municipality of Manatí is well known for its history as a cultural center in the early 20th _____ this distinction has earned it the fitting nickname of “the Athens of Puerto Rico.”'),
  ('a011ba0c-aab4-50ca-bafa-69b1ecbc5c1f', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["nanoparticles properties", "nanoparticle’s properties", "nanoparticles’ properties’", "nanoparticles’ properties"]'::jsonb, 15, 'Working on an unimaginably small scale of billionths of a meter, nanoengineers have found ways to leverage palladium ______ to facilitate energy generation in fuel cells.'),
  ('180d4aad-d895-5303-bab1-f29ce631f1a5', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["drum,", "drum:", "drum", "drum;"]'::jsonb, 16, 'Bertie Marshall, a key figure in the history of steel band music in Trinidad and Tobago, made several innovations to the steel ______ wheels so the instrument could be easily transported during Carnival, a cover to protect the pans from the sun, and amplification so the sound of the pans could be better heard over large crowds and other instrumentation.'),
  ('06c6fdeb-abbf-5b0d-85cc-a1d97fe7d505', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["compounds, hexafluorobenzene (C₆F₆) and benzene (C₆H₆),", "compounds hexafluorobenzene (C₆F₆) and benzene (C₆H₆),", "compounds, hexafluorobenzene (C₆F₆) and benzene (C₆H₆)", "compounds hexafluorobenzene (C₆F₆) and benzene (C₆H₆)"]'::jsonb, 17, 'In a chemical equation, the value known as molar mass is useful for converting between the mass of the reactants and the mass of the product. The liquid ______ have molar masses of 186.06 and 78.12 g/mol, respectively.'),
  ('df4c774f-59f9-5fc1-8126-6ea28578455d', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["was dictating", "dictated", "dictating", "dictates"]'::jsonb, 18, 'The statement "all tarantulas are venomous" is scientific because it could be proved false by a single observation to the contrary, according to Karl Popper . Popper’s theory ______ that scientific hypotheses must be refutable, termed the criterion of falsifiability, rejects the confirmationist position that uses verifiability as the standard for scientific hypotheses.'),
  ('d4372458-d9dd-5e4c-a635-11a2b5d31661', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["authors asserted", "authors were asserting", "authors, had asserted", "authors, asserting"]'::jsonb, 19, 'Geologist and scholar John Bostock joined with 55 other prominent British writers in 1837 to petition the US Congress for greater copyright protections. This cadre of renowned ______ that American publishers’ appropriation of their work caused, in the words of the petition, “deep and extensive injuries...on their reputation and property,” helped sow the seeds for the International Copyright Act of 1891.'),
  ('cb34020c-90dd-5819-89be-5d060e856bf4', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["To date,", "For example,", "In other words,", "Rather,"]'::jsonb, 20, 'In 2003, some 393 years after Galileo Galilei and Simon Marius independently discovered Jupiter’s four largest moons (Io, Europa, Ganymede, and Callisto), scientists discovered the moon Helike in orbit around Jupiter. ______ researchers have discovered eighty moons orbiting Jupiter.'),
  ('cac95abf-3264-5b42-b666-d0eb61903d09', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Instead,", "Furthermore,", "Thus,", "Similarly,"]'::jsonb, 21, 'The Hornbostel-Sachs system classifies musical instruments by how they produce sound. For example, an instrument that is popular in Norway called the hardingfele produces sound primarily through the vibration of its strings. ______ under the Hornbostel-Sachs system, the hardingfele is a chordophone.'),
  ('a4e1db79-6808-5b35-a17e-85122d71458b', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["conversely,", "specifically,", "likewise,", "thus,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'Brain imaging research led by neuroscientist Dwaynica Greaves found that actors showed suppressed responses in the left anterior prefrontal cortex (the portion of the brain associated with self-awareness) when their names were called during performances; ______ the actors’ responses were normal in nonacting contexts. These findings suggest that when embodying characters, performers may temporarily set aside their personal identities.'),
  ('0fd3a75b-68af-5e33-a7d6-8200b61db901', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to explain the 50 Completers hiking challenge to a new audience?', '["Hikers aiming to count themselves among the 50 Completers must reach not only West Virginia’s Spruce Knob but also the even higher peak of Mount Elbert in Colorado.", "Not until after you have reached the highest points in all fifty US states—including Spruce Knob in West Virginia and Mount Elbert in Colorado—can you include yourself among the 50 Completers of the Highpointers Club.", "If you are looking for a new hiking challenge, consider joining the Highpointers Club, as did In Suk Han, a hiker who successfully reached the highest point in every US state.", "On November 15, 2013, In Suk Han finally completed the feat of reaching the highest point in all fifty US states, including Spruce Knob in West Virginia and Mount Elbert in Colorado."]'::jsonb, 23, 'The Highpointers Club is a hiking club.
One of the main goals among club members is to reach the highest points in all fifty US states. Those who achieve this are called 50 Completers.
In Suk Han became a 50 Completer on November 15, 2013.
The highest point in West Virginia is Spruce Knob, at 4,863 ft.
The highest point in Colorado is Mount Elbert, at 14,440 ft.'),
  ('56ccff51-31c5-5407-a0bc-9154abee97e6', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to introduce Marie Hall to a new audience?', '["Of the 1,000 or so violins Antonio Stradivari made, only about 500 exist today.", "Marie Hall was a British violinist who once owned a Stradivarius violin.", "The Marie Hall Stradivarius is named after Marie Hall.", "Born in 1644, Antonio Stradivari was an Italian instrument maker whose violins are famous for their quality."]'::jsonb, 24, 'Antonio Stradivari (1644–1737) was an Italian instrument maker.
He made about 1,000 violins in his lifetime.
Musicians prize his Stradivarius violins for their famed sound quality.
The Marie Hall Stradivarius is named for former owner Marie Hall, a British violinist.'),
  ('be0cd714-d757-5514-b862-8a20e4ead243', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'The student wants to support Browner’s claim about Louis Ballard’s compositions. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["According to Browner, composer Louis Ballard, who wrote Desert Trilogy, relies on elements with origins in Native musical traditions.", "Browner posits that Louis Ballard’s compositions rely on elements originating within Native musical traditions; Washington Festival Suite affirms this assertion, incorporating a Pueblo log drum.", "Classical composer Louis Ballard, who wrote Washington Festival Suite, was nominated for the Pulitzer Prize in music in 1971.", "Browner claims that Louis Ballard’s compositions, one of which was nominated for the Pulitzer Prize, rely on elements originating within Native musical traditions."]'::jsonb, 25, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Louis Ballard was a classical composer and citizen of the Quapaw Tribe.
Ballard’s composition Desert Trilogy was nominated for the Pulitzer Prize in music in 1971. His composition Washington Festival Suite incorporates a Pueblo log drum, a traditional Native instrument.
Ethnomusicologist Tara Browner claims that Ballard’s compositions rely on elements originating within Native musical traditions.'),
  ('10119c4c-b5c7-5ff5-8804-6f431bbaa0eb', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'The student wants to present the study’s research methods. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["To further analyze the origins of Earth’s unique conditions, researchers focused on rocks from the Archean eon, when Earth’s temperatures were extremely high.", "Birner’s team analyzed the age and oxidation levels of rock samples collected from two seafloor ridges.", "Birner led a study to better understand the history of Earth’s mantle <u>and</u> explain the conditions that allowed life to develop.", "By studying these ancient rocks, the team aimed to challenge previous theories about changes in Earth’s mantle over time."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Suzanne K. Birner led a study analyzing rocks on the seafloor to better understand the history of Earth’s mantle.
Rock samples were collected from two seafloor ridges.
The researchers determined the samples’ period of formation (the Archean eon) and oxidation level (extremely low).
High temperatures in the Archean likely caused the rocks’ low oxidation.
Birner’s team suggests the oxidation of Earth’s mantle has remained stable over time, contrary to previous theories.
The findings help explain the unique conditions that allowed life to develop on Earth.'),
  ('fd9a35d1-37b4-5188-b2d4-71071a432540', '03b47ec3-38f5-5b7a-93ce-b1a809ac576d', 'The student wants to place the 1821 Declaration of Independence in the context of Valle’s changing political beliefs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["A change in Valle’s political beliefs that occurred when the Spanish king repealed the 1812 constitution led to Valle writing Central America’s Declaration of Independence.", "Long an opponent of Central American independence, Valle changed his mind after an 1820 revolt and wrote the 1821 declaration.", "The writing of Central America’s Declaration of Independence may not have happened were it not for Colonel Riego’s 1820 revolt.", "Colonel Riego’s revolt was the inspiration that Valle, a long-standing opponent of Central American independence, needed to change his political beliefs."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Leaders of the Province of Guatemala proclaimed independence for Central America from the Spanish Empire on September 15, 1821.
The accompanying Declaration of Independence was written by Honduran scholar and politician José Cecilio del Valle.
The 1812 Spanish Constitution had provided some degree of independence for Central America, but it was repealed by the Spanish king in 1814.
Valle, a loyal advisor to the Spanish Empire’s administrators in Central America, had long opposed independence.
He changed his mind after Colonel Rafael del Riego’s 1820 revolt, which demanded the return of rights lost in 1814.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('7d4a22a4-b970-5c57-88b1-a286a5705ead', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage describes a series that published novels anonymously and that invited readers to guess authors’ identities, emphasizing secrecy and mystery. The adjective "clandestine" means secret or concealed, so "This clandestine approach to publishing allowed Alcott to experiment beyond her established style" precisely and logically follows the context.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Preliminary" means initial or preparatory; nothing in the passage indicates the series was merely an early or preparatory stage, so it does not match the description of anonymity and mystery.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Unequivocal" means clear or unambiguous, which contradicts the passage’s focus on mystery and guessing authors’ identities.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Inexplicable" means unable to be explained; while the series provoked mystery, the passage specifically emphasizes secrecy and anonymity, not that the approach was beyond explanation.
**Faster approach:** Use context clues—words like "anonymously," "guess," and "mystery" point to a synonym meaning "secret"; eliminate choices that mean "clear," "initial," or "unexplainable."'),
  ('9e633301-1259-5cb8-90a7-a3ba8da0b331', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage describes an "underground" computer game created as an act of political protest under an authoritarian regime, so the most logical word to describe how copies were distributed is one meaning "secretly"; "surreptitiously" precisely conveys clandestine, covert distribution consistent with the historical and political context.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Disingenuously" means insincerely or deceptively in a moral or communicative sense, not simply "in secret," so it does not fit the intended meaning about the manner of distribution.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Succinctly" means briefly or concisely and refers to style or length, which is irrelevant to how copies were circulated.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Dispassionately" means without emotion or bias and does not describe a secretive mode of distribution, so it is not appropriate here.
**Faster approach:** Identify the clue word "underground" and match it to a choice meaning "secretly," eliminating options that describe tone or manner rather than secrecy.'),
  ('e36ea62e-4f51-538a-9f9d-24e8631ba8e0', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence requires a verb meaning “causes” or “gives rise to” because fragmentation is described as producing changes to local ecological processes that will affect specialist and generalist species differently. “Engenders” precisely means “causes” or “brings about,” so it completes the sentence logically and precisely.
**Choice B is incorrect.** “Preempts” means to take action in advance of or to prevent, which conflicts with the intended sense that fragmentation produces alterations rather than preventing them.
**Choice C is incorrect.** “Subsumes” means to include or absorb into something larger; that meaning does not fit the context of fragmentation producing alterations to ecological processes.
**Choice D is incorrect.** “Attenuates” means weakens or reduces; this would imply fragmentation lessens alterations, which contradicts the passage’s implication that fragmentation causes changes that affect species differently.
**Faster approach:** Quickly substitute each choice into the sentence and read for whether it conveys “causes” or “reduces/prevents” — the correct answer will be the verb meaning “causes,” so choose the synonym “engenders.”'),
  ('c6c551f4-bca3-580c-8285-65bfa56eb5fb', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence says that simply counting patents does not accurately represent how much innovation is happening because truly novel patents are rare compared with nonnovel ones. "Belies" means "gives a false impression of" or "contradicts," which fits precisely: tallying patents can misrepresent the degree of innovation.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Portends" means "predicts or foreshadows," which does not match the idea that counting patents produces a misleading impression about current innovation; it’s about signaling future events, not misrepresenting present reality.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Thwarts" means "prevents or obstructs." Counting patents does not prevent innovation; the sentence is about measurement, not interference.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Maligns" means "speaks badly of or defames." The act of tallying patents isn’t portrayed as criticizing innovation; it’s portrayed as giving an inaccurate picture, so "maligns" is wrong.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each choice into the sentence and ask whether the meaning matches "gives a misleading impression about the amount of innovation." Eliminate options that imply prediction, prevention, or criticism to quickly arrive at "belies."'),
  ('ee477269-05a6-5d86-ab6f-8b389bc28a93', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The underlined sentence uses the concessive word “despite” to introduce a contrasting fact: although García Márquez is popularly associated with magical realism, he also wrote works of strict realism (for example, Chronicle of a Death Foretold). That contrast directly refutes any impression that he exclusively wrote in the magical-realist mode.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The sentence does not compare García Márquez’s realist work to Castillo’s So Far from God or claim a similarity between them; it simply notes García Márquez’s realist output.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The sentence makes no claim about influence flowing from Mexican American authors back to García Márquez; it only reports that he wrote realistic works.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The sentence says nothing about the relative quality of One Hundred Years of Solitude versus García Márquez’s realist works; it only contrasts genre, not superiority.
**Faster approach:** Look for contrast markers like “despite” or “however” to identify sentences that counter or limit a preceding generalization — those typically serve to refute or qualify an impression.'),
  ('d2e449e8-3e72-5c21-8f84-d6baaf41ff0c', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** Text 1 reports findings from non-boreal ecosystems that warming tends to benefit invasive species over natives. Text 2 reports observations from Alaska (a boreal environment) showing that neither the native nor the invasive species varied in growth with warmer summers. That contrast suggests the pattern described in Text 1 may not apply in the boreal setting, so Text 2 casts doubt on the generalizability of the Text 1 research.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Text 2 does not describe a methodology to avoid a problem from Text 1; it reports observational results, not a methodological fix.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Text 2 does not offer an alternative explanation for the trend in Text 1; it reports counterevidence from a different environment rather than proposing a different cause.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Text 2 does not directly challenge the validity of the Text 1 findings (the original studies may be valid for non-boreal ecosystems); instead it questions whether those findings apply broadly to boreal systems.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the second text repeats, contradicts, or limits the first text; if it shows differing results from a different context, choose the option about generalizability.'),
  ('08582153-53d1-505f-9374-3fd1d34e548a', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage states that advances in language processing have “greatly boosted the utility” of translation apps for “perfunctory tasks” but that they “generally fail[] to convey many nuances” and are inadequate for “richer communication,” so the apps have improved but remain insufficient for complex interactions.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage says nothing about tourists specifically or about differential acceptance by business professionals; it discusses general utility and limitations, not who embraces them.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not claim the apps are becoming simpler to use or that their coverage of languages is inconsistent.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Although the passage credits impressive gains, it also describes clear practical uses (menu inquiries, passing interactions), so it does not say the apps are inadequate for most practical purposes.
**Faster approach:** Look for the contrast words (e.g., "be that as it may") that signal improvement plus a limitation; choose the answer that preserves both the progress and the specific limitation on complex interactions.'),
  ('0d73977e-aecc-568c-9737-837a93d9782b', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage says Mohol bushbabies forage more when lunar intensity is higher and explains that their greater reliance on vision means higher lunar intensity "benefits them more" than it benefits the woodrats. That language implies that any costs that lunar intensity might impose on bushbabies are outweighed by visual advantages, so disadvantages would be compensated for by advantages.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not claim that equal costs would produce no association; it emphasizes differential benefits (vision) rather than parity of costs.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage links the advantage specifically to reliance on vision, so it does not imply that non-vision-reliant species would share those advantages.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage explains a reason for the difference (vision reliance), so it does not support the idea that their behaviors should be more similar.
**Faster approach:** Identify the causal explanation in the passage, then eliminate choices that contradict that stated cause; pick the option that restates the direct implication.'),
  ('7b21bfb3-781e-5978-ba9f-6ff6740bd793', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The table shows that the biochar-and-compost treatment yields the largest increase in fruit weight and size among treatments that do not include inorganic fertilizer: weight = $979.3$ g (higher than biochar alone $915.7$ g and compost alone $864.8$ g), length = $6.76$ cm (highest of the three), and diameter = $13.68$ cm (also highest of the three). Thus farmers without access to inorganic additives would get the greatest gains by using biochar and compost together.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although biochar and NPK fertilizer give the largest weight ($1032.1$ g) and slightly larger dimensions, that choice requires inorganic NPK fertilizer, which the prompt excludes.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Biochar alone improves weight and size relative to control, but its weight ($915.7$ g) and dimensions are smaller than those for biochar and compost.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Compost alone produces the smallest improvements of the three organic options (weight $864.8$ g, length $6.15$ cm, diameter $13.22$ cm), so it is not the best choice.
**Faster approach:** Scan the table for treatments that exclude inorganic fertilizer, then compare their weight values first (largest weight indicates greatest overall increase); use length/diameter to break any ties.'),
  ('2eac602f-34ea-524a-8681-3f1cb6b07452', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The table shows that 58% of resolution makers ages 50–64 make personal-relationship resolutions, compared with 53% of those ages 30–49, and 56% of those ages 50–64 make finance resolutions, compared with 63% of those ages 30–49. Those two paired comparisons directly support the statement that 50–64-year-old resolution makers are more likely to make personal-relationship resolutions and less likely to make finance resolutions than 30–49-year-old resolution makers.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage says half of resolution makers make more than one resolution, which is not a majority, and the table gives no information specifically about whether people who choose health resolutions also choose additional categories.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The table reports percentages of types of resolutions among people who do make resolutions; it does not give overall rates of making any resolution by age, so the claim about all US adults is unsupported.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The table shows 79% of ages 18–29 choose health and exercise versus 80% of ages 30–49, so 18–29 are not more likely than 30–49 to make those resolutions.
**Faster approach:** Compare the relevant cells in the table directly for the two age groups named; for questions comparing likelihoods, a simple side-by-side read of the numeric percentages gives the answer quickly.'),
  ('78eab20a-5b84-5bb3-aaa3-a2f85715bd36', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The underlined claim says Beloved “resembles classic magical realist novels in its juxtaposition of literary realism with long-established cultural traditions—namely, those of the Black American community.” Choice D directly states that Beloved largely follows realistic conventions while also “incorporat[ing] elements drawn from Black American cultural traditions that transcend and expose the limitations of realism,” which mirrors the passage’s claim that Morrison pairs realism with cultural/antirealistic elements drawn from Black American traditions.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it mentions both Black American traditions and Latin American magical realism, it frames them as “competing influences” rather than saying Beloved juxtaposes realism with cultural traditions in the way the passage asserts.
**Choice B is incorrect.** This choice asserts that Black American traditions “figure so prominently in the magical realist tradition of Latin America,” which reverses the passage’s claim (the passage says Latin American magical realism influenced Beloved, not that Black American traditions are central to Latin American magical realism).
**Choice C is incorrect.** It emphasizes alternation between realistic and antirealistic modes but omits the key idea that Morrison pairs realism specifically with Black American cultural traditions in a way comparable to magical realism; it also implies the influence is merely constant rather than juxtaposed.
**Faster approach:** For command-of-evidence items asking which quotation supports a specific claim, identify the claim’s two key components (here: realism + Black American cultural traditions) and eliminate choices that reverse, omit, or change those components; the remaining choice will match the passage’s phrasing and inference.'),
  ('0d236575-8087-5ff9-91ae-f55f21f057ea', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The experiment showed that all ecotypes grew equally well in each of the four water samples and that added zinc increased growth for all ecotypes regardless of concentration. Those results rule out water‑related differences as the basis for any observed local advantage, so if the ecotypes are locally adapted, the adaptations must be to environmental factors other than the chemical composition of the water.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The data do not eliminate the possibility of local adaptation entirely; they only indicate that the water chemistry tested is not the source of such adaptations.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The zinc treatment enhanced growth for all ecotypes, so the results do not support the claim of significant differences in zinc resistance between ecotypes.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage points out that light and temperature were not replicated, but it gives no suggestion that the zinc levels used were inappropriate; D offers an unsupported alternative explanation rather than following from the reported results.
**Faster approach:** Use direct matching: identify what the results rule out (water differences) and eliminate answer choices that overreach or introduce unsupported claims; select the choice that makes the minimal, directly supported inference.'),
  ('2c63cd87-7845-5a42-8f7c-03ce37be0ab3', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage states that males prefer drab green females for mating but that a substantial minority (28%) of adult females retain juvenile blue plumage, despite receiving less mate preference; this suggests that the persistence of blue plumage is not explained by mate attraction. The researchers’ hypothesis that blue coloration is driven by factors other than mate attraction directly accounts for both the observed male preference and the continued existence of blue females.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Nothing in the passage supports a prediction that the percentage of blue-feathered females will increase over time; the data only describe current proportions and male preferences, not evolutionary trajectories.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage describes male behavior toward green versus blue adult females, not any failure of green females to distinguish others; the suggested inability to distinguish is unsupported and unnecessary for the observations.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage gives no information about males’ interactions with juveniles during resource competition, so we cannot infer their behavior toward juvenile jacobins.
**Faster approach:** Use elimination: match the hypothesis directly to both stated observations (male mate preference for green and persistence of blue females). The only choice that accounts for both is that blue coloration is driven by non-mating factors.'),
  ('59f1afad-f67e-5105-b598-3f72bf1ca1c9', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence contains two independent clauses: "In Puerto Rico, it’s not unusual... of Manatí is well known for its history as a cultural center in the early 20th" and "this distinction has earned it the fitting nickname of ''the Athens of Puerto Rico.''" Joining two independent clauses without a coordinating conjunction requires a semicolon, so "century;" correctly links them while maintaining standard punctuation.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "century and" creates an ungrammatical construction ("in the early 20th and this distinction…") because "and" would need a parallel structure or an additional clause before it.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "century" with no punctuation produces a run-on sentence by placing two independent clauses together without a proper connector.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A comma alone creates a comma splice, which is incorrect for joining two independent clauses without a coordinating conjunction.
**Faster approach:** Whenever two independent clauses are joined without a coordinating conjunction, use a semicolon; if you see a bare comma or no punctuation, watch for a run-on or comma splice.'),
  ('a011ba0c-aab4-50ca-bafa-69b1ecbc5c1f', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence needs a possessive form showing that engineers leverage the properties belonging to palladium nanoparticles. "Nanoparticles'' properties" is the plural possessive form (properties of nanoparticles) and fits grammatically and semantically: "leverage palladium nanoparticles'' properties to facilitate..."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Nanoparticles properties" lacks the required apostrophe to show possession and is ungrammatical in Standard English.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Nanoparticle’s properties" is singular possessive (properties of one nanoparticle); the context implies the general properties of palladium nanoparticles (plural), so the singular possessive is not the best choice.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Nanoparticles'' properties''" has an extra apostrophe at the end of "properties," which wrongly attempts to make "properties" possessive and is ungrammatical here.
**Faster approach:** Identify the noun that must possess "properties." If it refers to more than one nanoparticle, use the plural possessive "nanoparticles'' properties." Check apostrophe placement: after the plural noun (s'') for plural possession, not twice or missing.'),
  ('180d4aad-d895-5303-bab1-f29ce631f1a5', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** A colon is the proper punctuation to introduce the list of innovations that follows: wheels so the instrument could be easily transported during Carnival, a cover to protect the pans from the sun, and amplification. The clause before the blank is an independent clause, so a colon is the correct signal that a list will follow.
**Choice A is incorrect.** A comma does not appropriately introduce a multiitem list after an independent clause and would leave the sentence unclear.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Leaving no punctuation would make "steel drum wheels" read as a single compound noun and would fail to mark the following phrases as separate list items.
**Choice D is incorrect.** A semicolon links independent clauses or separates complex list items; it does not introduce a simple list here.
**Faster approach:** When a sentence signals multiple items (words like ''several'' or ''including''), expect a colon after the preceding independent clause to introduce the list; choose a colon rather than a comma, semicolon, or nothing.'),
  ('06c6fdeb-abbf-5b0d-85cc-a1d97fe7d505', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence requires a noun phrase ("compounds hexafluorobenzene (C₆F₆) and benzene (C₆H₆)") that directly completes "The liquid ____ have molar masses..." with no intervening comma after "compounds." In this construction, "compounds" is followed by the specific compounds serving as an essential appositive (identifying which compounds), so no comma should separate them from the noun they modify. The plural verb "have" correctly agrees with the plural subject "compounds hexafluorobenzene and benzene."
**Choice A is incorrect.** A comma after "compounds" creates a nonessential appositive, which would require the subject earlier to be complete; here the sentence reads awkwardly as if "compounds" stood alone as the subject and the named chemicals were parenthetical, which disrupts agreement and clarity.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Omitting the final comma while keeping the comma after "compounds" leaves a single, misplaced comma that still incorrectly sets off the named chemicals as nonessential when they are essential to identifying the subject.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Ending the phrase with a comma creates a terminal punctuation error inside the sentence (an unnecessary comma before the verb clause) and again treats the specific chemicals as parenthetical rather than the essential identification of the compounds.
**Faster approach:** When a noun is immediately followed by the words that identify it (an essential appositive), do not use commas. If the appositive is nonessential (extra information), set it off with commas. Use this rule to decide comma placement quickly.'),
  ('df4c774f-59f9-5fc1-8126-6ea28578455d', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence already contains the finite verb "rejects" ("...rejects the confirmationist position..."). To avoid having two independent finite verbs attached to the same subject without a conjunction, the clause that precedes "rejects" must be nonfinite and act as a modifier. The present-participial form "dictating" correctly makes "Popper’s theory dictating that scientific hypotheses must be refutable, termed the criterion of falsifiability, rejects..." a grammatical sentence in which the participial phrase and the participial modifier "termed..." modify "theory."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Was dictating" is a finite past-progressive verb form; pairing it with the present-tense finite verb "rejects" creates an ungrammatical sequence of two main verbs without coordination.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Dictated" is a past-tense (or past-participial) form that, here, would function like a second finite verb and conflict with the present-tense main verb "rejects," producing a tense/structure mismatch.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Dictates" is a second finite verb; placing it before the existing finite verb "rejects" yields two independent verbs with the same subject and no conjunction, which is ungrammatical in this construction.
**Faster approach:** Spot the sentence’s main finite verb (here, "rejects"). Any preceding clause that isn’t the main clause should use a nonfinite form (present participle or past participle) to avoid having two uncoordinated finite verbs—so choose the participial form.'),
  ('d4372458-d9dd-5e4c-a635-11a2b5d31661', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence already contains the main verb “helped” later on, so the phrase in the blank must modify “This cadre of renowned” rather than act as the clause’s main verb. "Authors, asserting that American publishers’ appropriation of their work caused...," is a nonessential participial phrase that correctly links the assertion to the cadre and allows "helped sow the seeds" to function as the sentence’s main action.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Authors asserted" supplies a finite verb; that would make "asserted" the main verb and leave the later verb "helped" with no clear subject, producing an ungrammatical double-verb structure.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Authors were asserting" also provides a finite verb phrase (progressive) that conflicts with the later main verb "helped," so it cannot correctly fill a modifying slot.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The comma plus past perfect "had asserted" is ungrammatical here: it creates a clause that doesn’t fit with the sentence’s intended modifier structure and interrupts the flow to the main verb "helped."
**Faster approach:** Scan for the sentence’s main verb (here, "helped"). If the blank appears before that main verb and must describe the subject, pick a noun phrase or participial modifier (not a finite verb) so the sentence keeps a single main verb.'),
  ('cb34020c-90dd-5819-89be-5d060e856bf4', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence that follows a specific event (the 2003 discovery of Helike) with a cumulative update about all discoveries calls for a phrase that signals an accumulated total; "To date," appropriately introduces the present total: "To date, researchers have discovered eighty moons orbiting Jupiter."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "For example," introduces an instance that supports a general claim; here the passage moves from a specific event to a cumulative statement, not offering Helike as an example of something broader.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "In other words," signals a restatement or clarification of the previous idea, but the second sentence gives new cumulative information, not a rephrasing.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Rather," indicates contrast or correction, which does not match the relationship between the two sentences.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the second sentence restates, contrasts, gives an example, or updates totals—"To date" is the stock phrase for updates about totals.'),
  ('cac95abf-3264-5b42-b666-d0eb61903d09', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence explains that the hardingfele produces sound by vibrating strings; therefore, the conclusion that it is a chordophone follows logically. "Thus" signals a logical result or conclusion, so it correctly connects the evidence about how the instrument produces sound to the classification implied by the Hornbostel-Sachs system.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Instead" signals a contrast or substitution, but the second clause does not contrast with the first; it draws a conclusion from it.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Furthermore" adds an additional fact, but the second clause is not an extra fact—it is the logical classification based on the preceding information.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Similarly" would indicate a comparison to something else, but no second item is being compared; the sentence states a conclusion about the same instrument.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the second clause is a conclusion, addition, contrast, or comparison; replace the blank with a short synonym (therefore/also/instead/similarly) to see which fits—here "therefore/thus" fits.'),
  ('a4e1db79-6808-5b35-a17e-85122d71458b', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence sets up a contrast: actors showed suppressed responses during performances, and the sentence that follows says the actors’ responses were normal in nonacting contexts. "Conversely" signals an opposite condition and so logically links the two clauses.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Specifically" narrows or gives a detail about the preceding statement, but the second clause presents a contrasting result, not a specific example.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Likewise" indicates similarity; it would imply the nonacting responses were also suppressed, which contradicts the sentence’s content.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Thus" indicates a conclusion or result drawn from the first clause, but the second clause describes a contrasting situation rather than a consequence.
**Faster approach:** For transition questions, identify whether the second clause contrasts, exemplifies, adds, or concludes relative to the first; then choose the transition that matches that logical relationship (contrast → conversely/ however; similarity → likewise; example → specifically; result → thus).'),
  ('0fd3a75b-68af-5e33-a7d6-8200b61db901', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** It most clearly conveys the club’s stated goal from the passage: membership as a 50 Completer requires reaching the highest point in each of the fifty U.S. states, and it uses Spruce Knob and Mount Elbert as concrete examples to illustrate that requirement. This both defines the challenge and gives representative examples for a new audience.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It mentions two specific peaks but focuses narrowly on those examples and does not state that hikers must reach the highest points in all fifty states.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It promotes joining and cites In Suk Han’s accomplishment but fails to define the 50 Completers challenge or the requirement to climb every state high point.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It summarizes In Suk Han’s completion and date but reads like a news item rather than explaining the club’s requirement to reach each state’s highest point.
**Faster approach:** For synthesis/explanatory items, choose the answer that explicitly states the rule or definition given in the passage and uses examples only to illustrate it; eliminate options that merely narrate or advertise.'),
  ('56ccff51-31c5-5407-a0bc-9154abee97e6', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** It introduces Marie Hall directly and efficiently by stating who she was and why she is connected to the topic: "Marie Hall was a British violinist who once owned a Stradivarius violin." This uses the passage information about her nationality and the instrument named for her to give a clear, relevant introduction to a new audience.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It offers a factual detail about Stradivari’s violins that does not introduce Marie Hall and would not help a reader learn who she is.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Though true, it merely restates that the instrument is named for her and does not provide any biographical information to introduce her to readers.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It focuses on Antonio Stradivari’s birth and reputation rather than introducing Marie Hall, so it fails to present her to a new audience.
**Faster approach:** For synthesis questions asking for the best introduction of a person, choose the answer that names the person and gives one clear, relevant fact about them (role, nationality, or relationship to the topic).'),
  ('be0cd714-d757-5514-b862-8a20e4ead243', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** It directly uses Browner’s claim and supports it with a specific example from the notes: the Washington Festival Suite incorporates a Pueblo log drum, which demonstrates that Ballard used elements from Native musical traditions.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It restates Browner’s claim but points to Desert Trilogy without showing how that piece supports the claim; the nomination fact does not provide evidence of Native elements.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It combines unrelated facts (classical composer, Pulitzer nomination) and does not address Browner’s claim about Native musical elements.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Mentioning a Pulitzer nomination does not provide evidence for Browner’s specific assertion about the use of Native musical elements.
**Faster approach:** Choose the option that pairs the claim with a concrete piece of evidence from the notes (a specific composition and a named Native instrument) rather than unrelated biographical facts.'),
  ('10119c4c-b5c7-5ff5-8804-6f431bbaa0eb', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** It directly states the research methods described in the notes: Birner’s team collected rock samples from two seafloor ridges and analyzed those samples’ ages (Archean eon) and oxidation levels. This choice focuses on the specific procedures the researchers used rather than their conclusions or motivations.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It emphasizes the Archean’s high temperatures and origins of Earth’s conditions, which is a conclusion about cause rather than a concise statement of the methods used to gather and analyze samples.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It combines Birner’s overall goals (understanding the mantle, explaining conditions for life) rather than describing the specific methods (what was collected and what was measured).
**Choice D is incorrect.** It states a research aim—challenging previous theories—not the concrete methods (collecting and analyzing rock samples) used in the study.
**Faster approach:** Scan the notes for verbs describing actions (collected, analyzed, determined) — those phrases usually name methods; pick the choice that repeats those action words.'),
  ('fd9a35d1-37b4-5188-b2d4-71071a432540', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The notes state that Valle was "long opposed" to independence, that he "changed his mind after Colonel Rafael del Riego’s 1820 revolt," and that he "wrote" the Declaration in 1821. Choice B accurately and succinctly places the 1821 declaration in the context of Valle’s changing political beliefs by linking his prior opposition, the 1820 revolt as the turning point, and his authorship of the 1821 document.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It attributes Valle’s change to the repeal of the 1812 constitution, but the notes say the constitution was repealed in 1814 and that Valle changed his mind after the 1820 revolt, not in 1814.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It speculates that the declaration “may not have happened” without the revolt; the notes imply a causal relationship but do not support this counterfactual level of speculation.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It asserts that the revolt was the "inspiration" Valle "needed," which adds interpretive language beyond the straightforward chronology given; the notes only state he changed his mind after the revolt.
**Faster approach:** Match answer choices to the explicit chronology and wording in the notes—identify statements that reflect the exact sequence (opposition → 1820 revolt → change of mind → 1821 authorship) and eliminate options that introduce unsupported causal or interpretive claims.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;

-- PeaklyTest Nov 2025 · Math · Module 1
